{
 "translation": "In vain, Bedi was hitting the ground with his stick and making a mournful sound, and somewhere among them, Sunderlal was going, tears still flowing from his eyes. Today, his heart was deeply hurt, and people were singing together with great enthusiasm: \"Hath laaiyaan kumhlaani lajavanti de boote.\" The sound of the song was still echoing in people's ears. It wasn't even morning yet, and the widow of Mullah Shakur's house in the neighborhood was still taking painful stretches in her bed when Sunderlal's neighbor Lalchand, whom Sunderlal and Khalifa Kalka Prasad had given a ration depot by using their influence, came running and, spreading his hands from his thick sheet, said, \"Congratulations, Sunderlal!\" Sunderlal, while putting sweet tobacco in the chillum, said, \"Congratulations for what, Lalchand?\" \"I saw Lajo Bhabhi!\" The chillum fell from Sunderlal's hand, and the sweet tobacco scattered on the floor. \"Where did you see her?\" He grabbed Lalchand by the shoulders and asked, and not getting an answer quickly, he shook him. Sundarlal let go of Lalchand at the Wagah border and said only this much, \"It must be someone else.\" Lalchand reassured him, \"No, brother, it was Lajjo herself. Lajjo! You even recognize her!\" Sundarlal again picked up the sweet tobacco from the floor, rubbed it in his palm, and asked, doing so, he picked up Risalo's chillum from the hookah and said, \"What is her identification?\" \"One tattoo is on her chin, another on her cheek.\" \"Yes, yes, yes!\" And Sundarlal himself said, \"The third is on her forehead.\" He didn't want any doubt to remain now, and suddenly he remembered all the familiar tattoos on Lajwanti's body, which she had gotten made on her body in childhood, which were like those light green seeds that are on the body of a touch-me-not plant, and which, as soon as you point to it, begins to wither. In exactly the same way, as soon as a finger was pointed at these tattoos, Lajwanti would become shy and disappear, as if all her secrets had become known to someone, and as if she had become impoverished by the looting of some unknown treasure. Sundarlal's whole body began to be thrown into an unknown fear, an unknown love, and its sacred fire. He again grabbed Lalchand and asked, \"How did Lajo reach Wagah?\" Lalchand said, \"There was an exchange of women between India and Pakistan, wasn't there?\"",
